For Claude/Codex/other coding agents, the **best requirement format is not a traditional BRD with long prose**. It should be a structured, machine-readable **Business + Functional Specification** where every requirement has an ID, explicit behavior, rules, inputs/outputs, state transitions, acceptance criteria, and test scenarios.

For your enterprise payment platform, I would use the following structure.

# AI-Ready Business Requirements Specification

## 1. Purpose

This document defines the business behavior, functional requirements, business rules, workflows, data requirements, integrations, security, recovery expectations, and acceptance criteria for the application.

The document must be written so that:

1. A business analyst can validate the business intent.
2. An architect can derive the system design.
3. A developer can implement the functionality.
4. An AI coding agent can locate the required implementation.
5. A QA engineer can derive automated functional tests.
6. An AI coding agent can verify implementation against the requirement.
7. Operations can understand expected processing and recovery behavior.

The requirements must be **specific enough to implement and test without guessing**.

---

# 2. Requirement Writing Principles

Follow these rules for every requirement.

### Rule 1 — Give every requirement a unique ID

Use stable IDs:

```text
BR-001
BR-002
BR-003

FR-001
FR-002

BUS-001
API-001
SEC-001
REC-001
```

Never use requirement numbers that change when the document is reordered.

---

### Rule 2 — One requirement should describe one behavior

Bad:

> The system should receive the file, validate it, process transactions, send messages, handle errors and generate the response.

Good:

```text
FR-001 File Registration
FR-002 File Validation
FR-003 Transaction Processing
FR-004 Reservation Request
FR-005 Response Processing
FR-006 Response File Generation
```

This makes implementation and testing much easier.

---

### Rule 3 — Avoid vague words

Avoid:

* quickly
* efficiently
* appropriately
* securely
* normally
* properly
* user-friendly
* high performance
* reliable
* seamless
* as needed

Replace them with measurable behavior.

Bad:

> The system should process files quickly.

Good:

> The system shall begin processing a registered file within 30 seconds of successful file registration under normal operating conditions.

---

### Rule 4 — Use "shall" for mandatory behavior

Use:

> The system shall...

Use:

> The system may...

for optional behavior.

Use:

> The system should...

only when the requirement is intentionally non-mandatory.

---

### Rule 5 — Separate requirement from implementation

Do not write:

> The system shall use PostgreSQL with Entity Framework Core.

unless that is actually a business/technical constraint.

Prefer:

> The system shall persist the transaction before sending it for reservation.

Then architecture/design documents can specify:

> PostgreSQL is used for durable transaction persistence.

This allows AI agents to distinguish **what the system must do** from **how it is implemented**.

