### My recommendation for your platform

For the ACH prefund/settlement application, I would **not put everything into one enormous BRD**. Use a small set of linked documents:

```text
docs/
├── requirements/
│   ├── 01-business-requirements.md
│   ├── 02-functional-requirements.md
│   ├── 03-business-rules.md
│   ├── 04-workflows-and-state-machines.md
│   ├── 05-integration-requirements.md
│   ├── 06-security-requirements.md
│   └── 07-recovery-and-multi-dc-requirements.md
│
├── architecture/
│   ├── system-architecture.md
│   ├── data-architecture.md
│   ├── messaging-architecture.md
│   └── deployment-multi-dc.md
│
├── testing/
│   └── requirements-test-matrix.md
│
└── verification/
    └── implementation-verification-report.md
```

The **single most important principle** is this:

> **Every requirement should have explicit acceptance criteria, and every acceptance criterion should have at least one executable test.**

That creates a chain an AI coding agent can reliably follow:

**Requirement → Acceptance Criteria → Code → Test → Build → Evidence → Verified**

For your use case, I would also add a **machine-readable YAML/JSON representation of each requirement** alongside the human-readable Markdown. That makes automated requirement parsing, traceability, gap detection, and AI-driven implementation significantly more reliable.
